GSS baseline for all adults is roughly 25% very happy, 57% pretty happy and 18% not too happy (2018); post-2020 figures run lower, near 19/56/25.

This group is likely mostly unmarried, since they are renters without children at home. Unmarried men report lower happiness than average. Moderate income and not receiving benefits partially offset that.

{"option_0": 18, "option_1": 59, "option_2": 23}

Young men without earnings who live in an owned home are mostly students or men living in their parents' house. They are not receiving benefits, so they are probably not deeply poor.

In recent GSS waves (2021–2022), young adults answer roughly 17% very happy, 58% pretty happy and 25% not too happy. Being unemployed or out of work pushes the "not too happy" share higher. Being a student moderates that effect.

{"option_0": 17, "option_1": 56, "option_2": 27}

Women aged 30-44 with no earned income are a mixed group. Many are homemakers, often married, and that subgroup tends to report being happy. Others are unemployed, disabled or low-income, and those people tend to be less happy.

Recent General Social Survey figures put the overall distribution at roughly 20% very happy, 56% pretty happy and 24% not too happy (2021-2022). Given that mix, I estimate this group sits a little below the overall average.

{"option_0": 24, "option_1": 52, "option_2": 24}

GSS benchmarks for adults 65+ (pre-2020): about 33% very happy, 53% pretty happy, 14% not too happy. Post-2020 figures are lower overall, around 24% very happy and 19% not too happy.

This group is men 65+ with no work income, so mostly retirees, with some disabled or low-income members. Retirees are generally fairly happy, which offsets some of the risk from those subgroups.

{"option_0": 29, "option_1": 55, "option_2": 16}

Women aged 45-64 with no earnings, no Social Security and no means-tested benefits, who own their homes, are mostly homemakers with working spouses or early retirees living on pensions or savings. That profile suggests relatively high marriage rates and financial security, both of which go with higher happiness. Some will instead be out of work because of disability without receiving SSDI, which pulls the other way.

General Social Survey (GSS) baselines are roughly 30% very happy, 55% pretty happy and 15% not too happy, with lower happiness in recent years. Married homeowners score higher than average, while non-workers in poor health score lower. Weighing those together, I estimate 32 / 53 / 15.

{"option_0": 32, "option_1": 53, "option_2": 15}

Men aged 30-44 with no earnings this year are likely unemployed, disabled without benefits, or stay-at-home fathers supported by a spouse's income. Owning a home and having children at home both point toward partnership and financial stability, which tends to raise happiness. Not working is associated with lower happiness among men, though.

As a baseline, the GSS for married people with children in this age range runs roughly 35% very happy, 55% pretty happy and 10% not too happy. Joblessness pushes these numbers down, so I estimate about 28% very happy and 18% not too happy.

{"option_0": 28, "option_1": 54, "option_2": 18}

This group is men aged 45-64 with no earnings, renting, living without children, and receiving neither means-tested benefits nor Social Security. Many are likely unemployed, disabled without SSDI, or early retirees with modest means. Renting and no job both point to lower happiness, and many are probably unmarried.

For reference, General Social Survey baselines recently run roughly 19% very happy, 56% pretty happy and 25% not too happy. Unemployment and non-homeownership shift answers substantially toward "not too happy," so I've lowered "very happy" and raised "not too happy" relative to that baseline.

{"option_0": 15, "option_1": 52, "option_2": 33}

Young men in this group are not working, rent, and receive means-tested benefits. Unemployment and low income are both strongly associated with unhappiness. In the GSS, about 30% of all adults say "very happy" and about 13% say "not too happy." Young adults have trended lower in recent years. Among the unemployed, "not too happy" rises to roughly 25-30%. Not being married lowers happiness further.

Estimate: very happy 16%, pretty happy 53%, not too happy 31%.

{"option_0": 16, "option_1": 53, "option_2": 31}

GSS-style happiness question. Recent national figures run roughly 19-25% very happy, 55-60% pretty happy, and about 20% not too happy. Higher income raises the very-happy share. Renting lowers it somewhat, and so does being a woman aged 30-44 with children (stress), though parents are about average overall. Married status is unknown; renters with kids at this income level include some single mothers.

My estimate: very happy 24%, pretty happy 60%, not too happy 16%.

{"option_0": 24, "option_1": 60, "option_2": 16}

GSS-style happiness item. Recent waves (post-2020) show overall US adults at about 19-24% very happy, 56-60% pretty happy, and 18-24% not too happy.

High income lifts happiness. Men aged 30-44 earning $150k+ are mostly married, which also raises it. Among top-income respondents, "very happy" is roughly 30-35% and "not too happy" roughly 8-10%.

{"option_0": 33, "option_1": 58, "option_2": 9}

Survey data on the general US population, such as the General Social Survey in recent years, puts the split at roughly 20-25% very happy, 55-60% pretty happy and 15-20% not too happy.

This group shows signs of financial strain that tend to lower happiness: they rent, receive means-tested benefits and have moderate incomes. Two factors pull the other way. Their personal earnings of $25k-$75k suggest they are employed, and having children at home has only a modest effect either way. Women are slightly happier than men on this measure. Overall I shift the general-population figures toward lower happiness.

{"option_0": 20, "option_1": 56, "option_2": 24}

Young women aged 18-29 with moderate earned income who are not on benefits should have relatively good self-rated health. This four-option scale, as used by Pew and GSS, typically gives young adults roughly 25-30% excellent, 50-55% good, 15% fair and 2% poor.

Young women in recent surveys report somewhat worse health than young men, likely tied to mental health. Being employed and not receiving benefits pushes the estimate toward better health.

{"option_0": 27, "option_1": 55, "option_2": 16, "option_3": 2}

Young men aged 18-29 with no earnings who are homeowners are most likely students living in family-owned homes, or people not working for other reasons. Some of the non-workers may have health problems that keep them out of work. However, this group receives neither Social Security (which would include SSDI) nor means-tested benefits (which would include SSI), so disability is less likely to be common.

As a baseline, young men in surveys using this four-point scale (such as Pew) typically answer roughly 30-35% excellent, 50% good, 13% fair and 2% poor. I shifted that slightly toward the lower categories to allow for the health-related share of non-workers.

{"option_0": 30, "option_1": 50, "option_2": 16, "option_3": 4}

Women aged 45-64 who have earned income, own homes, and receive no means-tested benefits or Social Security are mostly employed and middle class. That profile points to better-than-average self-rated health.

Using this four-point scale, which has no "very good" option, general US adult responses run roughly 20-25% excellent, 50% good, 20% fair and 5% poor. Being employed and middle-income shifts this group toward the healthier end, offset slightly by the older 45-64 age band, giving an estimate near:

{"option_0": 21, "option_1": 56, "option_2": 19, "option_3": 4}

Low earned income at age 45-64 often reflects part-time work, illness, or partial disability, which pushes self-rated health down. Against that, this group are homeowners, have children at home, receive no means-tested benefits and receive no Social Security (so no SSDI). That suggests some are self-employed or have a working spouse, which pulls health back up.

As a baseline, US adults aged 45-64 in a four-option version of this question split roughly 20% excellent, 50% good, 23% fair and 7% poor. Shifting that baseline for the low-earnings group gives the estimate below.

{"option_0": 17, "option_1": 48, "option_2": 26, "option_3": 9}

Working-age men aged 45-64 with moderate earned income are employed, which points toward better-than-average self-rated health. Being renters with modest income points the other way, so the two roughly offset. No means-tested benefits and no Social Security (including disability) both shift the group toward healthier respondents.

National benchmarks on this four-point scale (excellent/good/fair/poor, with no "very good" option) run roughly 20-25% excellent, 50% good, 20% fair and 5% poor. For this group I estimate about 20% excellent, 55% good, 21% fair and 4% poor.

{"option_0": 20, "option_1": 55, "option_2": 21, "option_3": 4}

This group is women aged 30-44 who rent, have children at home and report no earnings. Most likely they are stay-at-home mothers supported by a spouse's income. Some may be unable to work because of illness but not on disability benefits, since they receive no Social Security. They are also not on means-tested benefits, which suggests moderate household income.

The four-category question (excellent/good/fair/poor) is the Pew style. For the general adult population it typically runs about 20% excellent, 50% good, 23% fair and 7% poor. Younger women would usually look somewhat healthier than that. Two features pull the other way: renting and having no earnings are both associated with worse self-reported health, which raises the fair and poor shares.

{"option_0": 21, "option_1": 50, "option_2": 22, "option_3": 7}

Older women with some earned income are likely still partly working, which goes with better health. Homeownership and not receiving means-tested benefits point the same way.

The question uses a 4-point scale (excellent/good/fair/poor) rather than 5 points. On this 4-point format, which Pew uses, adults 65+ typically answer roughly: excellent 15%, good 50%, fair 27%, poor 8%. Given the working and better-off profile here, I shift slightly toward better health.

{"option_0": 17, "option_1": 53, "option_2": 24, "option_3": 6}

Self-rated health on this 4-point scale (no "very good" option) usually splits roughly as follows for US adults overall: about 25% excellent, 50% good, 20% fair and 5% poor.

Men aged 30-44 skew healthier than average because of their age. However, earning only $1-$24,999 is associated with worse health. Some in this group work part-time because of health limitations, while others are students or simply low earners. I've weighed these two pressures against each other in the estimate below.

{"option_0": 20, "option_1": 50, "option_2": 24, "option_3": 6}

Women aged 45-64 who rent, have no earnings and children at home, and receive neither means-tested benefits nor Social Security are a mixed group. It likely includes homemakers supported by a spouse's income, people living on other income, and some who are out of work because of disability but have not yet received SSDI. The lack of work income and the renting both point toward somewhat worse self-rated health than average. Not receiving benefits pulls the other way, since it suggests the group is not dominated by those with the most severe health or financial problems.

On this four-point scale, the general US population tends to answer roughly 20% excellent, 50% good, 22% fair and 8% poor. Adjusting for this group's characteristics shifts the distribution moderately toward fair and poor.

{"option_0": 14, "option_1": 44, "option_2": 29, "option_3": 13}

With a four-point scale (no "very good" option), the general population answers roughly 20% excellent, 50% good, 23% fair and 7% poor.

This group sits well above that baseline. They are high-income men aged 30-44, homeowners, with children at home and no benefit receipt. Income is a strong predictor of self-rated health, so I shift the distribution sharply toward the top: excellent rises to around 38-40%, good stays near 50%, fair drops to about 9%, and poor falls to about 1%.

{"option_0": 39, "option_1": 51, "option_2": 9, "option_3": 1}

Men aged 30-44 with no earnings this year are a mix of the unemployed, students, stay-at-home fathers and people with disabilities. Because this group receives no Social Security (so no SSDI) and no means-tested benefits (so no SSI or Medicaid), the most disabled people are likely underrepresented. Still, having no work income is associated with worse health. Renting points toward lower socioeconomic status.

As a baseline, Pew's four-point version of this question for the general population runs roughly 20–25% excellent, 50% good, 20% fair and 5% poor. For this group I shift toward worse health while keeping most respondents in "good."

{"option_0": 18, "option_1": 47, "option_2": 26, "option_3": 9}

Older men who are not working and receive means-tested benefits likely have lower incomes and worse health than other men their age. Having children at home suggests a multigenerational household, which often goes with lower income. Being homeowners offsets this somewhat.

As a baseline, older adults on this four-point scale typically answer roughly 12% excellent, 40% good, 33% fair and 15% poor. Shifting toward worse health for the low-income, benefit-receiving profile gives the estimate below.

{"option_0": 8, "option_1": 33, "option_2": 38, "option_3": 21}

Women aged 65+ who still earn $150k+ in personal income are mostly working professionals or executives. That points to strong health, since people in poor health are less likely to still be working at that level. The four-point scale used here (excellent, good, fair, poor) tends to put fewer people in "excellent" than the five-point version that includes "very good."

Comparable Pew data for high-income groups shows roughly 30-35% rating their health excellent. For older adults overall the split is about 20% excellent, 50% good, 23% fair and 7% poor. Shifting that baseline toward the healthier profile of high earners gives the estimate below.

{"option_0": 33, "option_1": 51, "option_2": 13, "option_3": 3}

Young men aged 18-29 with no earnings who are listed as homeowners are most likely students or others living in a parent-owned home. Their household finances therefore probably reflect their parents' situation. They also receive no means-tested benefits, which suggests they are not poor.

The GSS baseline for this satisfaction question is roughly 30% pretty well satisfied, 45% more or less satisfied and 25% not satisfied at all. Young people tend to be less satisfied. Having no job of their own pushes dissatisfaction up, while the comfortable household context pulls it down. Taken together, the estimate stays close to the baseline with slightly more dissatisfaction.

{"option_0": 27, "option_1": 46, "option_2": 27}

GSS data on financial satisfaction (SATFIN) gives the baseline. Overall, about 30% say "pretty well satisfied," 45% "more or less," and 25% "not at all." Adults aged 65+ report higher satisfaction, roughly 40%, 42% and 18%.

Within that age group, women with $25k–$75k in income are mid-level. Since earned income at 65+ often comes from people still working, this group sits around the middle of the older-adult range.

{"option_0": 39, "option_1": 44, "option_2": 17}

GSS data put adults aged 65+ at roughly 40% "pretty well satisfied," 40% "more or less satisfied," and 20% "not satisfied at all." Older adults generally report the highest financial satisfaction of any age group.

Two features of this group pull in different directions. Receiving means-tested benefits indicates low income, which pushes satisfaction down. Owning a home pushes it back up somewhat. On balance, I estimate a lower "pretty well satisfied" share and a higher "not satisfied" share than for seniors overall.

{"option_0": 30, "option_1": 44, "option_2": 26}

Men aged 30-44 with no earned income, renting, with children at home, and receiving no means-tested benefits or Social Security are likely unemployed or out of the labor force, which points to financial strain. Some may be students, stay-at-home fathers with a working spouse, or living on investment income, so not all are struggling.

The General Social Survey baseline for this question is roughly 30% pretty well satisfied, 45% more or less satisfied and 25% not satisfied at all. For an unemployed renter group, the "not satisfied" share would likely rise to about 40-45%.

{"option_0": 18, "option_1": 41, "option_2": 41}

This group is women aged 45-64 with no earnings, renting, on means-tested benefits, and not yet receiving Social Security. They are likely in financial hardship and possibly disabled or awaiting benefits.

In the GSS overall, the split runs roughly 30% pretty well satisfied, 45% more or less satisfied and 25% not satisfied at all. Low-income respondents shift heavily toward "not satisfied at all," probably to around 45-50%. For this group I estimate a small "pretty well satisfied" share, with the remainder split fairly evenly between the other two options.

{"option_0": 14, "option_1": 40, "option_2": 46}

Women aged 30-44 who own homes, have children at home, earn no wages and receive no benefits are likely stay-at-home mothers in single-earner, fairly affluent households. Such homeowners tend to lean somewhat conservative, and married stay-at-home moms more often identify as Republican.

General Social Survey support for the death penalty is roughly 60-63% favor among substantive answers in recent years. Women run lower, around 55%. The conservative tilt of this group pushes support back up somewhat, to roughly 58-60%.

{"option_0": 59, "option_1": 41}

Overall US support for the death penalty for murder runs about 55–60% in Gallup and Pew (GSS has been higher, around 60–65%). Men are more supportive than women, at roughly 60–65%.

This group is unusual: men aged 65+ who rent, have no earnings, and receive neither Social Security nor means-tested benefits. That combination likely includes immigrants not eligible for Social Security, some lower-SES people, and possibly some minority respondents, groups that tend to be less supportive. That pulls the estimate down somewhat from the male baseline.

{"option_0": 58, "option_1": 42}

GSS-style marijuana legalization question. Recent waves (2018–2022) show older adults aged 65+ at roughly 50–55% support. Women support legalization somewhat less than men. Two other traits point toward lower support: having children at home at this age (possibly grandchildren, a more traditional household) and renting.

Taken together, these suggest support around 48%.

{"option_0": 48, "option_1": 52}

GSS evidence points to low social trust among young adults, roughly 20% saying most people can be trusted. Women tend to report somewhat less trust than men. Moderate income and not receiving benefits push trust up slightly. Renting pushes it down slightly. "Depends" is a volunteered answer and usually draws about 3–5%.

Taken together, that puts "most people can be trusted" at about 22%, "can't be too careful" at about 73%, and "depends" at about 5%.

{"option_0": 22, "option_1": 73, "option_2": 5}

Older adults in the GSS tend to be more trusting than average, at roughly 40–45% "most people can be trusted." Receiving means-tested benefits signals lower income, and lower income is associated with lower trust. Homeownership pulls slightly in the other direction. Older white men in particular show relatively higher trust, but this group's low income offsets much of that. "Depends" is volunteered by about 4–5% of respondents.

On balance, I estimate about 33% choosing "can be trusted."

{"option_0": 33, "option_1": 62, "option_2": 5}

GSS-style trust question. Recent overall figures are roughly 25-30% "most people can be trusted," 65% "can't be too careful," and about 5% "depends."

This group skews toward higher trust: middle-aged men with high income, homeowners, and likely more education. Trust rises with income and education, so I'd put this group at roughly 40-45% trusting. I estimate 42 for "most people can be trusted," 52 for "can't be too careful," and 6 for "depends."

{"option_0": 42, "option_1": 52, "option_2": 6}

GSS "fefam" item: recent waves show about 25-30% of all adults agreeing. Women aged 65+ are more traditional, likely around 35-40% agreeing in recent years. Those with no wage or self-employment income (retired, older homemakers) lean slightly more traditional still.

My estimate for agreement is about 38-40%, with strong agree around 8%. Disagreement splits into roughly 37% disagree and 23% strongly disagree.

{"option_0": 8, "option_1": 31, "option_2": 38, "option_3": 23}

GSS fefam item: recent overall agreement is about 25%, and strongly agree runs around 5%.

This group is women aged 30-44 with children, low earnings, renters, and not on benefits. Many are likely part-time workers or stay-at-home mothers with low earned income, possibly married to a working spouse. That profile raises traditional views somewhat. Being renters and younger women pulls agreement back down. On balance I'd put agreement at about 28%.

{"option_0": 7, "option_1": 21, "option_2": 44, "option_3": 28}

Women aged 45-64 with no earned income, who own homes, have children at home and receive no benefits are likely homemakers in breadwinner households. That points to more traditional views than average.

In the GSS, about 25% of all US adults agree with this statement and about 75% disagree. Among homemakers the share agreeing is higher, perhaps 35-40%.

{"option_0": 9, "option_1": 30, "option_2": 40, "option_3": 21}

GSS data for the FEFAM item puts recent overall agreement at about 25-30%. Among men aged 30-44 it is somewhat higher, around 30%.

This group has some features that likely push agreement up. Men with low personal earnings who own homes and have children at home may partly be in families where the wife is the main earner. Others may be self-employed, or from more traditional or religious households with larger families. Mixed effects are plausible, but on balance I estimate agreement of roughly 33%.

{"option_0": 8, "option_1": 25, "option_2": 43, "option_3": 24}

GSS data on this item (FEFAM) shows older men agreeing at higher rates than younger adults. Among adults 65+ in recent years, roughly 35-40% agree. Older men run slightly higher, around 40-45%.

This group has no wage income and no Social Security, which suggests early retirees or people living on pensions or other income. They are likely somewhat affluent and possibly better educated, which would pull agreement down somewhat. I estimate about 38% agreeing overall.

{"option_0": 9, "option_1": 30, "option_2": 42, "option_3": 19}

This group is men aged 30-44 receiving Social Security with no earnings, which points to SSDI recipients: disabled, likely lower education, possibly older within the age band. Homeowners without children at home.

In the GSS, about 25-30% of all adults agree with this statement overall in recent years. Men aged 30-44 sit around 25%. Disabled people with lower education lean somewhat more traditional, so I estimate about 30% agreement for this group.

{"option_0": 7, "option_1": 24, "option_2": 45, "option_3": 24}

GSS "fefam" item: recent overall figures are roughly 5% strongly agree, 20% agree, 45% disagree, 30% strongly disagree.

This group (women aged 45-64, low income, renters) leans a bit less educated, which pushes toward more agreement. Being women without children at home and working pushes the other way. These roughly offset, so I estimate shares close to the overall figures.

{"option_0": 6, "option_1": 20, "option_2": 44, "option_3": 30}

GSS "fefam" item, recent waves: about 25% of all adults agree, with roughly 5% strongly agreeing and 20% agreeing. Disagree runs around 45% and strongly disagree around 30%.

For this group, men are somewhat more traditional than women. Men aged 30-44 earning $75k-$149k tend to be more educated, but many are married to a homemaker spouse, which pushes slightly toward agreement. I estimate total agreement near 27%.

{"option_0": 6, "option_1": 21, "option_2": 46, "option_3": 27}

GSS evidence on the "fefam" item (man as achiever, woman at home) shows about 25-30% of all adults agreeing in recent years. Agreement is higher among people aged 65+, at roughly 35-40%.

This group is narrower: older women with high personal earned income. Most of them are still working or recently retired professionals. That profile points toward lower agreement, around 20-25%, and a large share choosing "strongly disagree."

{"option_0": 5, "option_1": 18, "option_2": 42, "option_3": 35}

GSS FEFAM in recent years (2018–2022): about 25% agree or strongly agree and about 75% disagree or strongly disagree. Typical splits are roughly 5% strongly agree, 20% agree, 45% disagree, and 30% strongly disagree.

Homeowners are slightly older and more conservative than the general population, which pushes agreement up a bit. My estimate for homeowners is 6% strongly agree, 21% agree, 45% disagree, and 28% strongly disagree.

{"option_0": 6, "option_1": 21, "option_2": 45, "option_3": 28}

Older male homeowners on Social Security lean conservative. Their low earned income likely reflects retirement rather than poverty, so it should not shift them much toward the liberal end.

In general-population data, roughly 25–30% place themselves at 4 (moderate), about 9% at 2 (liberal) and about 3% at 1 (extremely liberal). Men aged 65 and over sit more conservative than that baseline.

{"option_0": 3, "option_1": 8, "option_2": 9, "option_3": 27, "option_4": 15, "option_5": 27, "option_6": 11}

Women aged 45-64 who rent, have no earnings, and receive neither means-tested benefits nor Social Security are a mixed group. It likely includes people with disability income not counted as Social Security, people living on pensions or support from others, and some with lower socioeconomic status.

Renters and unmarried people lean somewhat liberal, while lower-education respondents tend to answer "moderate." Women overall lean slightly liberal. On that basis I put the largest share at the midpoint, a modest liberal tilt on the left side, and a meaningful conservative share on the right.

{"option_0": 5, "option_1": 13, "option_2": 11, "option_3": 37, "option_4": 12, "option_5": 15, "option_6": 7}

This group is men aged 30-44 who own homes and have children at home but report no earned income and receive no benefits or Social Security. That profile is likely a mix of stay-at-home dads, disabled men not on Social Security, people living off spouse income, investors, and the unemployed.

Homeownership and having children tilt the group somewhat conservative. Being male also tilts conservative. Nonworking status may pull slightly toward the moderate category. The general ANES pattern for comparable groups puts about 26% at moderate.

{"option_0": 3, "option_1": 10, "option_2": 10, "option_3": 29, "option_4": 15, "option_5": 23, "option_6": 10}

Profile: women aged 30-44 with no earnings who receive means-tested benefits but no Social Security, so possibly disabled or out of the labor force. Homeownership points to somewhat lower income stress, and having no children at home matters too.

Lower-income women tend to choose "moderate" more often. Being younger than average tilts slightly liberal, while homeownership tilts slightly conservative.

{"option_0": 5, "option_1": 12, "option_2": 11, "option_3": 38, "option_4": 12, "option_5": 15, "option_6": 7}

Middle-aged women who rent, have no earnings and receive means-tested benefits are low-income and plausibly include many people with disabilities. Such groups are disproportionately Black or Hispanic and lean Democratic. Low-income respondents also tend to choose "moderate" heavily on ideology scales. Some white low-income women in this group would be conservative.

My estimate is about 38% moderate, about 30% on the liberal side and about 32% on the conservative side, giving roughly balanced tails around a large middle.

{"option_0": 6, "option_1": 12, "option_2": 11, "option_3": 38, "option_4": 11, "option_5": 14, "option_6": 8}

