GSS confidence in the executive branch, recent years (roughly 2018–2022), runs about 12–15% "a great deal," 40–45% "only some," and 40–45% "hardly any."

Older men tend to be somewhat more polarized and more likely to say "hardly any," though they lean Republican, so their answers depend on who holds the presidency. Pooled across years, this group likely lands near: 14% "a great deal," 40% "only some," and 46% "hardly any."

{"option_0": 14, "option_1": 41, "option_2": 45}

GSS figures for confidence in the executive branch, all years pooled, run roughly 14% "a great deal", 46% "only some" and 40% "hardly any". In recent years the "hardly any" share has risen to about 45–50%.

This group is lower-income women aged 30–44 who rent. That profile likely leans slightly toward "hardly any" and away from "only some".

{"option_0": 13, "option_1": 45, "option_2": 42}

GSS results for confidence in the executive branch, pooled across years, run roughly 13–15% "a great deal", 45% "only some" and 40% "hardly any".

This group is older, lower-income women who rent, receive Social Security and get means-tested benefits. Those traits point to:
- a higher share of Black and Democratic respondents, so answers depend on who holds the presidency;
- less education overall;
- more alienation from institutions, which raises "hardly any".

Older respondents sometimes give "a great deal" more often. Balancing these, I estimate:

{"option_0": 15, "option_1": 43, "option_2": 42}

This is the GSS confidence-in-the-executive-branch item. Across recent years the general population splits roughly 12% "a great deal," 45% "only some," and 43% "hardly any."

The group is men aged 30–44 with no work income who are receiving Social Security. At that age, this most likely means disability benefits (SSDI) or survivor benefits. People on disability tend to report lower institutional trust, so I shift the distribution toward "hardly any."

{"option_0": 12, "option_1": 40, "option_2": 48}

GSS-style question on confidence in the executive branch. Recent General Social Survey waves show roughly 12% "a great deal," 45% "only some," and 43% "hardly any" among all adults. High-income men aged 45-64 tend to be slightly more Republican, so their confidence depends on which party holds the presidency, and the survey year is unknown. Renters with children at home in this income band likely include more urban professionals, which pushes the other way.

Balancing these, my estimate stays close to the national figures, with a slight shift toward "a great deal" for this high-income group.

{"option_0": 13, "option_1": 47, "option_2": 40}

GSS confidence in the scientific community: roughly 40-48% say "a great deal," about 45% "only some," and 7-10% "hardly any." In the 2022 wave, after the post-COVID decline, it was about 39% great deal, 48% only some, 13% hardly any.

Young men with moderate income who rent may lean somewhat more independent or skeptical. Young adults also tend to show slightly lower "great deal" responses.

{"option_0": 38, "option_1": 49, "option_2": 13}

GSS confidence in the scientific community: overall, recent waves show about 40% "a great deal", 47% "only some", and 13% "hardly any".

